// **`allows_guard` is asked of the walk, not of the declaration.** a guard statement ends in a block, so
// it may only appear where a statement may - and Parser::parse_varexpr has five callers, of which
// exactly one reads a body. the other four are a parameter list, a struct body, and a `for` header's
// init and its step, none of which can be followed by a block
//
// so the `for` header's declaration never takes the guard branch, and the `guard` reaches
// Parser::parse_expr_ref - which says what a guard is and where it goes. the same shape the
// `static`-in-a-body refusal has: the caller is what knows where it is

function halve(int32 $n) : int32?
{
    if ($n < 0) {
        return null;
    }

    return $n / 2;
}

for (int32 $i = guard halve(10) else { break; }; $i < 10; $i++) {
    echo $i;
}
